lf._batch_size:\n            self._event.set()\n        else:\n            await self._event.wait()\n            self._event.clear()\n            self._curr_tasks = 0\n\n        print(\"Finished task\")\n\nactor = AsyncActor.options(max_concurrency=2).remote(2)\n\n# Only 2 tasks will run concurrently.\n# Once 2 finish, the next 2 should run.\nray.get([actor.run_task.remote() for _ in range(8)])\n\n\n[D3] (objectrefs-as-asynciofutures)=\n### Object refs as `asyncio.Future` objects\nYou can use object refs as `asyncio.Future` objects, which means you can `await` Ray futures in existing concurrent applications.\n\nInstead of:\n\n```{testcode}\nimport ray\n\n@ray.remote\ndef some_task():\n    return 1\n\nray.get(some_task.remote())\nray.wait([some_task.remote()])\n```\n\nyou can await the object ref in Python 3.9 and 3.10:\n\n\nClaim:\nBoth statements hold: (a1) The `max_concurrency` setting of a running Ray actor can be changed after the actor is created. (a2) Calling `future()` on a Ray object reference allows it to be used with `concurrent.futures` APIs.\n\nJSON verdict:",
